WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
Build-time secret handling is a common Docker image failure point.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:19.320
ASP.NET Core builds may need NuGet feed credentials, package registry tokens, signing keys, Git credentials, cloud credentials, certificates, connection strings, API keys, or private package source credentials.

3
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.300
These values should not become part of the image.

4
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.300
Do not pass secrets through Dockerfile ENV or ARG instructions.

5
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:34.920
Those values can persist in image history, build metadata, cache records, logs, or troubleshooting output.

6
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.580
A build argument may feel temporary, but it is not the same as a protected build secret.

7
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.020
When a build truly needs temporary secret access, use build-secret mechanisms supported by the build platform.

8
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:58.520
The goal is for the restore or build step to access the secret just long enough to complete the required operation without writing it into layers, package configuration files, final artifacts, or routine logs.

9
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.920
NuGet restore is a common place to review.

10
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:12.100
Feed credentials should be scoped narrowly, short-lived where possible, and provided through the CI or build platform in a way that avoids committing credentials to repository files.

11
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.600
Restore output should not print sensitive feed URLs or tokens.

12
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.680
Publish steps should also be reviewed.

13
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:28.980
Generated configuration, transformed files, static assets, and appsettings variants should not embed production secrets into the final image.

14
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:36.600
Runtime configuration should be supplied through the deployment platform or secret store according to the application's operational model.

15
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:48.400
CI/CD logs, cache exports, build summaries, provenance metadata, and troubleshooting screenshots can all expose build-time values if teams are not careful.

16
00:01:48.500 --> 00:02:00.180
Safer habits include short-lived credentials, managed build identities, least-privilege package access, redaction, cache controls, and periodic review of build records.
